1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,886
You're standing on hot sand in front of fifty thousand screaming people.

2
00:00:02,886 --> 00:00:05,773
Somewhere in that crowd, a man is deciding whether you live or

3
00:00:05,773 --> 00:00:08,660
die.

4
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:11,224
Half the crowd paid to see you win. The other half paid

5
00:00:11,224 --> 00:00:13,489
to see you bleed.

6
00:00:13,789 --> 00:00:16,409
And you didn't sign up for this. Well. Technically you signed something.

7
00:00:16,409 --> 00:00:19,029
We'll get to that.

8
00:00:19,329 --> 00:00:21,370
Let's go back to how you got here.

9
00:00:21,670 --> 00:00:24,914
And stick around till the end, because the hardest fight of your

10
00:00:24,914 --> 00:00:28,160
life isn't in the arena. It's what happens when you win.

11
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:31,640
You're born on the edge of the Roman Empire, in a small

12
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:34,820
village the Romans would describe as, not Roman enough.

13
00:00:35,119 --> 00:00:38,850
Which is a problem, because one day the Romans decide your village

14
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:42,580
needs to be a lot more Roman. Or a lot less existing.

15
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:47,895
You're captured. And in Rome, a prisoner of war becomes property. Most

16
00:00:47,895 --> 00:00:52,910
gladiators started exactly like this. Prisoners, slaves, and criminals.

17
00:00:53,210 --> 00:00:56,790
At the slave market, a buyer looks you over. Not your face.

18
00:00:56,790 --> 00:01:00,370
Your shoulders. Your teeth. Your legs.

19
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:04,705
He's a lanista. A gladiator manager. Think football club owner, except the

20
00:01:04,705 --> 00:01:08,740
players are his property, and the club is a prison.

21
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:12,474
Then comes the oath. Gladiators swore to endure being burned, bound, beaten,

22
00:01:12,474 --> 00:01:15,909
and killed by the sword.

23
00:01:16,209 --> 00:01:19,513
Some free men actually volunteered for this. Poor men, men in debt,

24
00:01:19,513 --> 00:01:22,816
men who wanted fame. They signed up willingly and took the exact

25
00:01:22,816 --> 00:01:26,120
same oath.

26
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:29,780
Which tells you something about how bad normal life could be.

27
00:01:30,079 --> 00:01:33,646
And yes, sometimes gladiators fought back. More than a hundred years before

28
00:01:33,646 --> 00:01:37,212
you, a gladiator called Spartacus broke out of his school with about

29
00:01:37,212 --> 00:01:40,777
seventy others and started a war that terrified Rome. It ended very

30
00:01:40,777 --> 00:01:44,343
badly for them. Your lanista has heard the story. Your door has

31
00:01:44,343 --> 00:01:47,909
a very good lock.

32
00:01:48,209 --> 00:01:51,765
Your new home is a ludus, a gladiator school. It's a training

33
00:01:51,765 --> 00:01:55,320
camp, a dormitory, and a jail, all in one.

34
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:58,745
You get a tiny cell. No windows worth mentioning. Somewhere to sleep

35
00:01:58,745 --> 00:02:01,870
between beatings.

36
00:02:02,170 --> 00:02:06,644
Training starts immediately. Wooden swords. Wooden posts. Hours and hours of the

37
00:02:06,644 --> 00:02:11,119
same moves until your body does them without thinking.

38
00:02:11,419 --> 00:02:14,685
Your trainers are often veteran gladiators. Men who survived long enough to

39
00:02:14,685 --> 00:02:17,949
teach. They are not gentle.

40
00:02:18,250 --> 00:02:22,139
You're assigned a fighting style. Each type had different armour and weapons,

41
00:02:22,139 --> 00:02:26,030
and they were matched against each other to make the fights interesting.

42
00:02:26,330 --> 00:02:29,580
The net and trident guy. The heavy shield guy. The fast guy

43
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:32,829
with almost no armour. It's basically choosing a character class, except the

44
00:02:32,829 --> 00:02:36,079
game is real.

45
00:02:36,379 --> 00:02:40,543
Fun fact. There were even a few female gladiators. Rare, controversial, and

46
00:02:40,543 --> 00:02:44,706
the Roman upper class found them shocking. Which, coming from people who

47
00:02:44,706 --> 00:02:48,869
watched men die for fun, is quite something.

48
00:02:49,169 --> 00:02:53,079
And what do they feed you? Barley. And beans. And more barley.

49
00:02:53,079 --> 00:02:56,989
Gladiators were nicknamed hordearii. Barley men.

50
00:02:57,289 --> 00:03:01,343
We actually have evidence for this. In nineteen ninety-three, archaeologists found a

51
00:03:01,343 --> 00:03:05,396
gladiator cemetery at Ephesus, in modern Turkey. Chemical tests on the bones

52
00:03:05,396 --> 00:03:09,449
showed they ate a mostly plant-based diet.

53
00:03:09,750 --> 00:03:13,267
And the same tests suggest they drank a special recovery drink. Made

54
00:03:13,267 --> 00:03:16,784
of plant ash. Ancient sources say gladiators drank it to recover after

55
00:03:16,784 --> 00:03:20,302
training. You are the world's first sports drink customer, and it tastes

56
00:03:20,302 --> 00:03:23,819
like a fireplace.

57
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:28,389
The good news? Your bones were probably stronger for it. The bad

58
00:03:28,389 --> 00:03:32,659
news? Stronger bones are mostly useful for getting hit more times.

59
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:35,704
Here's something surprising. You get good medical care. Some of the best

60
00:03:35,704 --> 00:03:38,449
in the Roman world.

61
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:41,496
The famous doctor Galen actually worked as a physician for gladiators early

62
00:03:41,496 --> 00:03:44,243
in his career. He later said it was where he learned the

63
00:03:44,243 --> 00:03:46,989
most about wounds.

64
00:03:47,289 --> 00:03:50,163
Why so much care? Because you are expensive. A dead gladiator is

65
00:03:50,163 --> 00:03:53,036
money in the ground. Your owner doesn't love you. He loves his

66
00:03:53,036 --> 00:03:55,909
investment.

67
00:03:56,210 --> 00:04:00,333
And here's something most movies get wrong. Gladiators didn't fight every day.

68
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:04,456
Many probably fought only two or three times a year. The rest

69
00:04:04,456 --> 00:04:08,579
of the time? Training. Waiting. Thinking about the next one.

70
00:04:08,879 --> 00:04:12,449
And then, after months of training, it's your first fight.

71
00:04:12,750 --> 00:04:15,914
The night before the games, gladiators were given a big public feast.

72
00:04:15,914 --> 00:04:19,079
People came to stare at you while you ate. Some men enjoyed

73
00:04:19,079 --> 00:04:22,245
it. Some couldn't touch the food. Some used it to say goodbye,

74
00:04:22,245 --> 00:04:25,409
and to make their wills.

75
00:04:25,709 --> 00:04:28,913
All over town, the fights were advertised by painting notices on the

76
00:04:28,913 --> 00:04:32,116
walls. Your name might be on one. Like a concert poster. Except

77
00:04:32,116 --> 00:04:35,319
the band might die.

78
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:38,870
And the games run all day. In the morning, wild animal hunts.

79
00:04:38,870 --> 00:04:42,120
Around lunchtime, criminals were executed. And in the afternoon, the main event.

80
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:45,370
You.

81
00:04:45,670 --> 00:04:49,375
The crowd is huge. There's music. Yes, music. Horns and even a

82
00:04:49,375 --> 00:04:53,079
water organ played during the fights.

83
00:04:53,379 --> 00:04:57,060
You fight. It's fast. It's exhausting. And everyone around you is shouting

84
00:04:57,060 --> 00:05:00,740
advice they would never follow themselves.

85
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:03,725
And there's a referee. Gladiator fights had rules, and a referee with

86
00:05:03,725 --> 00:05:06,409
a stick to enforce them.

87
00:05:06,709 --> 00:05:09,954
Now, the big question. Did every fight end in death? No. Gladiators

88
00:05:09,954 --> 00:05:13,199
were too expensive for that.

89
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:17,475
Historians estimate that in the early empire, something like one fight in

90
00:05:17,475 --> 00:05:21,449
ten ended with a death. Later, it got worse.

91
00:05:21,750 --> 00:05:25,540
If you lose but fought well, the crowd can ask for mercy,

92
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:29,329
and the man paying for the games decides. Thumbs up, thumbs down?

93
00:05:29,329 --> 00:05:33,120
Historians aren't actually sure which gesture meant what.

94
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:36,185
So your life depends on a hand signal that nobody, even today,

95
00:05:36,185 --> 00:05:38,949
can fully explain.

96
00:05:39,250 --> 00:05:42,497
And if mercy is refused? A defeated gladiator was expected to accept

97
00:05:42,497 --> 00:05:45,745
the final blow with courage. No begging. No running. The crowd wanted

98
00:05:45,745 --> 00:05:48,992
the death to look brave. Even your ending is part of the

99
00:05:48,992 --> 00:05:52,240
show.

100
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:56,870
But if you win? You become famous. In Pompeii, someone scratched graffiti

101
00:05:56,870 --> 00:06:01,199
about a gladiator named Celadus, saying he made the girls sigh.

102
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:05,810
You have fans. Rich women. Kids who play gladiators in the street.

103
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:10,120
Your face, sort of, on cheap little lamps and souvenirs.

104
00:06:10,420 --> 00:06:15,120
Some sources even say the sweat and oil scraped off gladiators' skin

105
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:19,819
was sold as a beauty product. You are, possibly, a skincare brand.

106
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:23,680
And yet, after every fight, you go back to the same tiny

107
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:27,240
cell. Famous, adored, and still somebody's property.

108
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:30,850
Gladiators were so famous that one emperor, Commodus, actually fought in the

109
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:34,159
arena himself. Of course, his opponents were not exactly allowed to win.

110
00:06:34,159 --> 00:06:37,469
Imagine being told to lose to your boss, but in a way

111
00:06:37,469 --> 00:06:40,779
where you don't die.

112
00:06:41,079 --> 00:06:43,620
Back to that cemetery at Ephesus. The skeletons tell us how this

113
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:46,159
usually ends.

114
00:06:46,459 --> 00:06:50,204
Many of the gladiators buried there died in their twenties. One skull

115
00:06:50,204 --> 00:06:53,949
had three holes in it. The exact spacing of a trident.

116
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:57,273
And many of their bones show old wounds that had healed. Proof

117
00:06:57,273 --> 00:07:00,296
that they were patched up, sent back out, and patched up again.

118
00:07:00,296 --> 00:07:03,319
Until one day they weren't.

119
00:07:03,620 --> 00:07:07,633
Some gladiators even had gravestones paid for by their wives or friends.

120
00:07:07,633 --> 00:07:11,646
Short messages. A name, an age, the number of fights. A whole

121
00:07:11,646 --> 00:07:15,659
life written in a few lines of stone.

122
00:07:15,959 --> 00:07:19,233
But you're one of the lucky ones. After years of wins, you

123
00:07:19,233 --> 00:07:22,506
receive a wooden sword. It's called the rudis. It means you're free.

124
00:07:22,506 --> 00:07:25,779
You'll never have to fight again.

125
00:07:26,079 --> 00:07:28,413
You walk out into the world. You're thirty-ish, covered in scars, and

126
00:07:28,413 --> 00:07:30,746
the only thing you've ever been good at is fighting in front

127
00:07:30,746 --> 00:07:33,079
of a crowd.

128
00:07:33,379 --> 00:07:37,659
No job. No family. No skills anyone will pay for.

129
00:07:37,959 --> 00:07:42,079
And so, like some freed gladiators really did, you go back. Same

130
00:07:42,079 --> 00:07:46,199
arena. Same sand. Except now you're doing it for money.

131
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:49,886
Or maybe, if you're lucky, you become a trainer. You spend the

132
00:07:49,886 --> 00:07:53,273
rest of your life teaching scared young men the exact moves that

133
00:07:53,273 --> 00:07:56,659
kept you alive. And watching some of them not come back.

134
00:07:56,959 --> 00:08:01,225
So that's your life. Captured. Sold. Fed barley, trained like an animal,

135
00:08:01,225 --> 00:08:05,490
cheered like a celebrity, and stitched up like an investment.

136
00:08:05,790 --> 00:08:08,350
You finally won your freedom, and found out the world outside the

137
00:08:08,350 --> 00:08:10,909
arena had no place for you.

138
00:08:11,209 --> 00:08:13,876
Thanks for living it with us. Which life should we live next?

139
00:08:13,876 --> 00:08:16,543
Tell us in the comments. And subscribe, so you don't miss your

140
00:08:16,543 --> 00:08:19,209
next past life.
